<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- The Log Viewer's data layer, lifted out of DevToolbox.Services so a headless host can
       reference it WITHOUT linking the rest of the toolbox.

       This exists for one reason, and it is a guardrail rather than tidiness. DevToolbox.Services
       carries Microsoft.PowerShell.SDK (in-process script execution) and the hosts-file editor
       including its elevated-command path. DevToolbox.Mcp is called by an AI agent, and the
       outermost defence against a capability being reached is the absence of a code path to it —
       so the agent-facing process must not have those assemblies loaded at all, registered as
       tools or otherwise.

       DEPENDENCY DIRECTION IS LOAD-BEARING: DevToolbox.Services references THIS project, never the
       reverse. A reference back would re-link everything this separation exists to exclude. This
       project must depend on nothing of ours.

       NAMESPACES ARE DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED (DevToolbox.Services.Models / .Interfaces / .Services).
       The assembly boundary and the namespace boundary are allowed to disagree, and here they must:
       keeping the namespaces means the extraction required ZERO source edits in DevToolbox.UI,
       DevToolbox.Tests or anywhere else, which is what makes it provably behaviour-preserving
       against a suite of 804 tests and a release that shipped the same day. Renaming them would
       have turned a project-file change into hundreds of source edits with no functional gain. -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="YamlDotNet" Version="13.7.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Mirrors DevToolbox.Services: the moved types keep whatever visibility the tests already
         relied on, so the extraction cannot break a test by tightening access. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DevToolbox.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
